## Data for this card from shift 6 (read-only measurement) — the defect is wider than `profiles.delete`

While finishing `fcf65219` I read the transport layer end to end. The swallow is **not** a `profiles.delete` quirk; it is `CodxJuniorConnection.delete()` itself.

`src/api/connection.js:142-148`:

```js
  delete(url) {
    ...
    const request = this.axiosInstance.delete(this.prepareUrl(url), { headers: this.headers })
    return this.trackLocalCall('DELETE', url, request)
          .finally(() => this.liveRequests--);
  }
```

Every other verb unwraps the body — `get` L81-88, `post` L111-118, `getBlob` L99-109, `postBlob` L120-132, `put` L137-140 all end with `.then((response) => response?.data)`. `delete()` alone returns the raw **AxiosResponse**, and `trackLocalCall` (L68-79) is documented to return *the same promise, untouched*.

Why the error path does not save you either: `interceptors` is `{request: [], response: []}` (L5-8), `api.js:163-165` exposes only a **setter** (`Object.assign`), and no `push` into `interceptors.response` exists in the reachable tree. So axios rejects on 4xx/5xx and nothing converts the error body into a resolved envelope.

Net for this card: **`profiles.delete` cannot "swallow the envelope" for the reason stated — the envelope never arrives.** Any caller inspecting `response.status_code` on a DELETE result (as `EffortsSettings.vue:376` now does) reads `undefined` on a 200-with-envelope refusal and treats it as success. Duplicate copy at `del(url)` L90-96 has the same omission; `api.js:113-114` `_send` dispatches DELETE to `delete`.

One-line fix, for whoever has a patch tool: add `.then((response) => response?.data)` to `delete()` (and `del()`), then `profiles.delete` / `efforts.delete` / admin deletes all receive bodies instead of AxiosResponse objects.

Evidence limits: no shell and no `node` in this runtime, so nothing was executed; the claims above are `file:line` quotes from `src/api/connection.js` (149 lines, all read) and `src/api/api.js`. No files changed by me. Cross-reference: `fcf65219` (in Review) carries the acceptance test that proves the branch is unreachable today.

